WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:01.320
Sam, one of our founding engineers,

2
00:00:01.320 --> 00:00:02.900
got me this amazing mug,

3
00:00:02.900 --> 00:00:04.450
which says on it,

4
00:00:04.480 --> 00:00:06.220
we do these things not because they are easy,

5
00:00:06.220 --> 00:00:08.250
but because we thought they would be easy.

6
00:00:08.520 --> 00:00:12.030
That highlights our naivete in the early days,

7
00:00:12.300 --> 00:00:13.400
thinking that this was a reasonable

8
00:00:13.400 --> 00:00:15.670
set of constraints for us to have.

9
00:00:16.520 --> 00:00:18.830
And so we built the first system in Postgres.

10
00:00:18.880 --> 00:00:21.490
We were like, cool, we're geniuses, we're so smart.

11
00:00:21.760 --> 00:00:22.880
Obviously, the thing to do,

12
00:00:22.880 --> 00:00:24.010
you toss it all in Postgres,

13
00:00:24.180 --> 00:00:25.570
it's normalized out,

14
00:00:25.920 --> 00:00:28.900
you filter over JSONB columns on the CRM data,

15
00:00:28.900 --> 00:00:31.200
and then you do a join

16
00:00:31.200 --> 00:00:32.730
over this web scale data,

17
00:00:32.740 --> 00:00:34.290
this event data, basically.

18
00:00:34.740 --> 00:00:35.120
And that worked

19
00:00:35.120 --> 00:00:38.430
for our first seven months of customers,

20
00:00:38.480 --> 00:00:40.370
and it got progressively slower and slower.

21
00:00:40.920 --> 00:00:42.720
And our database CPU utilization

22
00:00:42.720 --> 00:00:44.760
got progressively higher and higher

23
00:00:44.760 --> 00:00:47.060
until it got to a tipping point

24
00:00:47.060 --> 00:00:49.060
where our database was just running

25
00:00:49.060 --> 00:00:52.850
at 90 plus percent CPU utilization all the time.

26
00:00:53.620 --> 00:00:54.970
Very scary place to be at.

27
00:00:55.160 --> 00:00:56.470
Oh my God, this is crazy.

28
00:00:56.620 --> 00:00:57.560
Who could have seen this coming?

29
00:00:57.560 --> 00:00:59.480
And then you zoom the graph out

30
00:00:59.480 --> 00:01:00.520
over the six months,

31
00:01:00.520 --> 00:01:02.220
and it was a very steady line

32
00:01:02.220 --> 00:01:03.100
up and to the right

33
00:01:03.100 --> 00:01:04.870
until it hit 100%

34
00:01:05.180 --> 00:01:06.350
and then pinned at 100%.

35
00:01:06.980 --> 00:01:08.040
So the answer was

36
00:01:08.040 --> 00:01:09.530
we could have seen it coming,

37
00:01:09.620 --> 00:01:10.710
and we should have seen it coming.

38
00:01:12.780 --> 00:01:16.730
I spent six weeks writing and rewriting our,

39
00:01:16.780 --> 00:01:19.200
we had this kind of query building engine

40
00:01:19.200 --> 00:01:22.220
that translated customer queries,

41
00:01:22.220 --> 00:01:24.170
the UI, no SQL,

42
00:01:24.540 --> 00:01:25.780
no code SQL builder

43
00:01:25.780 --> 00:01:26.800
that they would have in the UI

44
00:01:26.800 --> 00:01:29.600
into this very complex SQL query

45
00:01:29.600 --> 00:01:31.650
that would run against Postgres in the back end.

46
00:01:31.660 --> 00:01:33.200
I did a bunch of optimizations for it.

47
00:01:33.200 --> 00:01:34.520
I was really deep in the query planner

48
00:01:34.520 --> 00:01:36.420
and what's the optimization fences

49
00:01:36.420 --> 00:01:37.120
and how to get

50
00:01:37.120 --> 00:01:39.100
the table stats to be just right,

51
00:01:39.100 --> 00:01:40.090
et cetera, et cetera.

52
00:01:40.520 --> 00:01:42.400
And that kind of kept us alive

53
00:01:42.400 --> 00:01:43.730
week by week.

54
00:01:43.860 --> 00:01:45.240
One day of optimization,

55
00:01:45.240 --> 00:01:47.320
two days of optimizations would buy us

56
00:01:47.320 --> 00:01:48.620
a couple more days at a time

57
00:01:48.620 --> 00:01:50.460
while Sam was really taking

58
00:01:50.460 --> 00:01:51.540
a new approach at it,

59
00:01:51.540 --> 00:01:52.380
which was, okay,

60
00:01:52.380 --> 00:01:53.260
if we're gonna let you filter

61
00:01:53.260 --> 00:01:55.310
on any field on any record,

62
00:01:55.800 --> 00:01:56.720
why don't we use

63
00:01:56.720 --> 00:01:59.020
a reverse index over this, right?

64
00:01:59.020 --> 00:02:00.940
So what's like the great classic reverse index

65
00:02:00.940 --> 00:02:01.890
is Elastic Search.

66
00:02:02.440 --> 00:02:03.540
And so we took all of this data

67
00:02:03.540 --> 00:02:05.300
and we dumped it on an Elastic Search

68
00:02:05.300 --> 00:02:09.030
and we built a new query layer on top of it

69
00:02:09.860 --> 00:02:12.320
to generate these elastic queries

70
00:02:12.320 --> 00:02:16.680
and use that to build these very complex reports

71
00:02:16.680 --> 00:02:18.470
that our customers needed.

72
00:02:18.580 --> 00:02:19.440
That was really the core

73
00:02:19.440 --> 00:02:20.840
and the heart of our product

74
00:02:20.840 --> 00:02:22.200
to run the automations

75
00:02:22.200 --> 00:02:23.230
that I described earlier.

76
00:02:24.020 --> 00:02:26.810
And I'm cranking away, getting us Postgres level optimizations.

77
00:02:27.360 --> 00:02:29.460
Sam gets this thing out and it works great.

78
00:02:29.460 --> 00:02:30.310
It works beautifully.

79
00:02:30.960 --> 00:02:32.090
We're like, we're geniuses.

80
00:02:32.160 --> 00:02:32.870
We solved this.

81
00:02:33.600 --> 00:02:34.590
Seven months later,

82
00:02:35.860 --> 00:02:37.050
growing very quickly,

83
00:02:37.220 --> 00:02:38.570
20% month over month,

84
00:02:38.620 --> 00:02:41.350
things like that and more than that many months.

85
00:02:41.640 --> 00:02:44.240
And our Elastic Search cluster

86
00:02:44.240 --> 00:02:47.010
can't keep up with the indexing load

87
00:02:47.400 --> 00:02:49.450
coming into the cluster.
